 112 | \section{The adaptive Bernstein--Vazirani bound}
 113 | The secret is $s\in\F_2^n$ and $f_s(x)=s\cdot x$ for $x\in\F_2^n$, with the dot product taken modulo two. Exact success means recovering the entire secret.
 114 | 
 115 | \begin{lemma}[Recorded monomial bound]\label{lem:degree}
 116 | For an algorithm with at most $q$ queries, its total terminal record $\Psi_s$ can be written
 117 | \begin{equation}\label{eq:monomials}
 118 |  \Psi_s=\sum_{\substack{J\subseteq\{1,\ldots,n\}\\|J|\le q}}
 119 |        \left(\prod_{j\in J}s_j\right)c_J
 120 | \end{equation}
 121 | in a fixed finite direct sum of terminal spaces. The coefficient vectors $c_J$ do not depend on $s$.
 122 | \end{lemma}
 123 | \begin{proof}
 124 | The root state has degree zero. On Boolean inputs in a characteristic-two field, $f_s(x)=\sum_js_jx_j$ as a scalar equality, hence
 125 | \[
 126 |  O_s=O_0+\sum_{j=1}^n s_j A_j,
 127 |  \qquad A_j=\bigoplus_x x_j(G_0+G_1).
 128 | \]
 129 | Multiplication by a query raises degree by at most one. Every other map on a fixed recorded branch is linear and independent of $s$ and therefore cannot increase degree. Reduce powers using $s_j^2=s_j$; this cannot increase total degree. Induct along each path in the finite tree. Although the chosen next operation depends on the history, that operation is fixed on the particular path and its map is independent of $s$. Impossible histories contribute zero coordinates, not removed coordinates.
 130 | 
 131 | Each terminal record thus has a multilinear expression of degree at most its number of queries, at most $q$. Concatenate coefficients for the same monomial across all terminal spaces, padding absent coefficients with zero. This gives \eqref{eq:monomials}. Early termination adds no query; no oracle-dependent padding or branch normalization is used. Ancillas enlarge each coefficient vector but do not add monomials.
 132 | \end{proof}
 133 | 
 134 | \begin{theorem}[Exact Bernstein--Vazirani complexity]\label{thm:bv}
 135 | Every exact algorithm in the pointwise characteristic-two contract requires $q\ge n$. With QXOR the exact complexity is $n$.
 136 | \end{theorem}
 137 | \begin{proof}
 138 | The terminal spaces are grouped by output label $s$. Exactness puts $\Psi_s$ entirely in its own label group, and Lemma~\ref{lem:record} makes it nonzero. The $2^n$ vectors $\Psi_s$ are consequently linearly independent. By Lemma~\ref{lem:degree}, however, they lie in the span of at most
 139 | \[
 140 |  D(n,q)=\sum_{j=0}^{\min(q,n)}\binom nj
 141 | \]
 142 | coefficient vectors. For $q<n$, $D(n,q)<2^n$, a contradiction. For the upper bound, standard XOR queries at the $n$ unit addresses $e_j$ give $f_s(e_j)=s_j$ on separate basis targets. Read them out with retained workspace.
 143 | \end{proof}
 144 | 
 145 | This is a polynomial-method specialization, not a competing lower-bound method. In complex-amplitude computation, the Boolean parity of secret bits is not their degree-one scalar sum in $\mathbb C$. Thus the proof does not contradict the ordinary one-query quantum Bernstein--Vazirani algorithm. Likewise, replacing exact operational readout with inspection of a full symbolic state removes the independence argument and changes the task.
