 166 | \section{Kickback without a Fourier analyzer}\label{sec:phase}
 167 | This section changes scalar domains explicitly: set $A=\A$, use free $A$-modules, balance tensors over $A$, admit invertible $A$-linear gates, and test possibility by nonzero contraction. It is a worked algebraic boundary, not an automatic extension of the field-query lower bounds to all ring algorithms. Let $R=1+u$ and $z=R^2=1+u^2$, so $R^4=z^2=1$.
 168 | 
 169 | \begin{proposition}\label{prop:phase}
 170 | For $\chi=(1,z)^T$ and the standard target flip $X$, $X\chi=z\chi$. Nevertheless
 171 | \[
 172 |  H_z=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&z\end{pmatrix}
 173 |        \sim\diag(1,u^2)
 174 | \]
 175 | is singular. Its $n$-fold tensor over $A$, viewed by regular representation over $\F_2$, has rank $4+2n$ for $n\ge1$ and dimension $4\cdot2^n$.
 176 | \end{proposition}
 177 | \begin{proof}
 178 | $X\chi=(z,1)^T=z(1,z)^T$. Adding the first row to the second and then the first column to the second gives the displayed diagonal form; these are invertible elementary operations. Tensoring those operations gives diagonal factors $u^{2k}$, one for each subset of $k$ positions. Since $u^4=0$, only $k=0,1$ contribute. Multiplication by $1$ on $A$ has binary rank four; multiplication by $u^2$ has rank two. There is one first factor and $n$ second factors, giving $4+2n$.
 179 | \end{proof}
 180 | 
 181 | \input{phase_table}
 182 | 
 183 | The character obstruction is elementary. A multiplicative character of $C_2^n$ into a characteristic-two field satisfies $a^2=1$, hence $(a+1)^2=0$ and $a=1$. Over $A$, unit-valued characters can be nontrivial, but every value reduces to $1$ modulo $(u)$. A square character matrix of size greater than one therefore reduces to the all-ones matrix and has nonunit determinant. This concerns an order-two group in characteristic two, not all finite-field transforms or odd-order groups.
 184 | 
